=== Reproducibility correction, 2026-08-18 (AMB-123 round 14) ===
Nothing above is retracted. What follows corrects how it can be CHECKED.

Two of the re-checks above cite artifacts that a reader cannot open:

  - "Arithmetic re-check (python3 script, run 2026-08-15): 17/17 checks PASS"
    names no file at all.
  - "Re-run: python3 <scratchpad>/fixround2-verify.py ... Raw output in
    scratchpad/fixround2-out.log" names a path under a per-session
    scratchpad directory. That directory is transient and is gone; the
    script and its output went with it.

So the two tallies -- 17/17, and 34 checks / 31 PASS / 3 FAIL -- are not
reproducible by anyone, including their author. That is exactly the standard
AMB-120 was filed to enforce, and this file sits in the same directory as the
logs that issue fixed. It is also the only tracked file in the repository that
names a session scratchpad path, which AGENTS.md forbids outright.

The reproducible artifact is tests/benchmarks/check-hand-assertions.py. It
re-derives A1-A8 from assertions.yaml, power-tree.yaml and the part records,
reconciles design.md figures and every published margin against them,
recomputes the shown work in this file and power-tree.yaml, runs under
`make all`, and
prints:

  hand-assert: PASS: esp32s3-devboard: 8 assertion(s) follow from their inputs; 7 design.md figure(s) reconcile against them; 10 published margin(s) are the gate's own slack; 39 power-tree figure(s) agree; 42 shown-work claim(s) recompute.

It also names the two it cannot mechanise and why -- A9_strapping_dc_state
(a boot-mode truth table, not a numeric relation) and A10_single_pin_nets_l9b
(a claim about what the DSL must declare, for the L9b lint rule under AMB-52).

That summary line used to read "11 assertion(s) follow from their inputs",
summing the two populations. There are ten assertions, so eleven of them was
never possible; three of the eleven were document figures. Worse than the
wrong noun: one total means a newly reconciled figure silently pays for a
deleted assertion. Split in the same commit as this correction.

Read the tallies above as a record of what was done on 2026-08-15, not as
evidence you can re-run. The evidence you can re-run is the gate.

=== Correction, 2026-08-19 (AMB-123 round 18) ===
The TVS leakage load carried 2 uA with no source; Littelfuse publishes only
IR max = 400 uA at VRWM 5.0 V for the SMF5.0A, with no typical, so the max
now stands in every mode row (the one load where the "datasheet typ" policy
has no typ to use). Re-derived: A2 VBUS worst sum 0.39491 -> 0.39531 A,
margin +105.1 -> +104.7 mA; sleep/idle/rx mode sums move by the same
+398 uA; voltage-propagation figures unchanged at their quoted precision.
The 2026-08-15 sections above are left as written (their arithmetic was
correct over the inputs they had). VBUS max 5.25 V sits above the 5.0 V
standoff, where leakage is unspecified entirely -- noted in power-tree.yaml,
not modelled.

=== Correction, 2026-08-19 (AMB-123 round 19) ===
The round-18 section above says the voltage-propagation figures were
unchanged at their quoted precision. That is true of the four propagated
voltages (3.818 / 4.335 / 4.585 / 4.903 V) and false of the margins DERIVED
from them, which the same edit should have moved: A4's in-house guardband
margin is +0.256 V, not the +0.257 V it kept publishing (3.81816 - 3.5618 =
0.25636; the +0.257 belonged to the retracted 2 uA-leakage current), and the
usbc_min secondary row is +0.773 V, not +0.774 V. Both are corrected in
assertions.yaml, and design.md's "+257 mV margin" with them. A4's inline
derivation also still showed the 2 uA-era drops (0.1382 / 0.0434, "exact
3.81834") where power-tree.yaml already showed 0.1384 / 0.0435; it now shows
the same intermediates as the tree.

Why it survived a round: nothing read shown work. calc_rows, secondary_rows
and calc_* were prose to every gate, so an arithmetic row could contradict
its own operands indefinitely. check-hand-assertions.py now recomputes each
named shown-work row at the precision the row itself publishes, and holds
A4's and A5's voltage inputs to the power-tree rows they transcribe by name
-- the current totals alone were reconciled before, which is why a Vf edit
could flip A4's physical verdict with every gate green.

=== Correction, 2026-08-21 (AMB-123 round 20) ===
Round 19 gave assertions.yaml's shown work a reader and stopped one file
short: power-tree.yaml publishes the same kind of derivation -- the mode
totals and the voltage propagation this log calls the benchmark's original
evidence -- and only the trailing "= X V" was read, so a row could state
work its own operands do not produce. Every arithmetic row in both files is
recomputed now, both directions (a row cannot leave the population by being
deleted, or join it by being added), each with the count of claims it
publishes. The same round-19 fix bound three of the four propagated
voltages; usbc_min, which A4's secondary row carries as a bare opening
literal, was the fourth and is bound now. No arithmetic in either file
turned out to be wrong -- every one of the 40 claims recomputes -- but none
of it was checked.

Two further populations were too small. design.md's reconciled figures were
three; the two dropout margins A4 exists to publish, the 391.6 mA peak rail
demand every current row derives from, and the 0.654 W that is the direct
operand of the gated 121.9 C were outside them, so the document could
contradict the assertion that computes it. And every `margin:` in this spec
was replaceable with an arbitrary number: A2's, A3's and A6's are now the
slack the gate itself computes, and the three that are not a single slack
(A1's two-sided interval, A5's two thermal bases, A7's ratio) are declared
as prose rather than left silently unread.

VBUS maximum. The source model takes 500 mA from the USB Type-C spec and
5.25 V from USB 2.0 -- the more favourable figure from each. Type-C vSafe5V
is specified 4.75 to 5.5 V, and at 5.5 V the datasheet minimum-pad junction
temperature would be 132.7 C, above this design's own 125 C gate (96.6 C at
the 62 C/W pour assumption). A5 remains gated at 5.25 V; the basis and the
derived corner are stated in power-tree.yaml rather than left implicit, and
choosing which bound this benchmark should model is AMB-126.

=== Correction, 2026-08-21 (AMB-123 round 21) ===
The shown-work reader shipped last round compared each claim against a
tolerance taken from its mantissa's decimals with the exponent ignored, so
`281e-6` was checked to +/-0.5 A: 15 of the 40 claims -- every engineering-
notation row in this tree -- were being compared at between 1% and 555,556%
of their own value, and a 1400x error in a mode total passed. The precision
a row publishes now includes its exponent. Nothing in either file turned out
to be wrong; the checks were.

Two further ways a claim went unread are closed. Units written INSIDE an
expression (`0.500 A - 0.39531 A`) made a fragment unevaluable, and an
unevaluable claim was a silent skip rather than a failure -- so a falsehood
could be hidden in a row whose recorded claim count asserted full coverage.
And the population was strings containing `=`, which four of the five T10
margin rows do not, so they could not even join it. Any string carrying a
number is in the population now, prose rows named at zero claims.

The T10 margins themselves -- including this benchmark's own "-> T10 PASS"
verdict -- are reconciled against each mode's total and the declared source
capability, both the milliamps and the percentage.

The data layer is no longer decoration. sources/edges/loads were read by
nothing while every summation restated them as literals, so the table and
the arithmetic claiming to sum it could disagree silently; each summation's
terms are now bound to the loads they sum, with the three figures that
deliberately differ (the LED taken at VBUS max, twice, and the dropped 1 uA
pull-up leakage at the two coarse branches) named with their reasons. And
every numeric assertion input is classified: bound to the tree path it
copies, or declared as coming from somewhere else. A1's rail interval, A3's
worst rail load, A6's PTC hold current and A7's predicted deep-sleep current
were transcriptions of tree rows that answered to nothing.

A3 recorded 0.39166 A as the worst 3.3 V rail load. That is the LDO INPUT
current (the p3v3 total plus the 60 uA Iq that flows Vin->GND); its own expr
binds the operand to sum(p3v3_loads), which is 0.39160 A, and A4 records the
same physical quantity correctly. Corrected. The 60 uA error was in the
conservative direction and no verdict moves.
